WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:02.540
INTRO:
You know how gas prices have come down substantially

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:08.029
over the past few months forom over $5 per
gallon to just under $4 per gallon?

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:13.679
Now of course, $4 per gallon is still significantly
higher than where we were just a year ago,

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:25.340
but it is nonetheless still progress from
$5 per gallon.

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:28.900
This has caused many to assume that supply
chain issues have been resolving and that

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.420
inflation has been cooling down and so on.

00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:35.670
And while these factors definitely do play
a role, one of the most blatant reasons for

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.780
the recent decrease is oil stimulus from the
US.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:43.059
You see, the US department of Energy likes
to keep a back up oil reserve called the US

00:00:43.059 --> 00:00:44.670
Strategic Petroleum Reserve.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:49.620
This reserve is meant for serious emergencies
like the US getting into a war or the Middle

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:50.850
East staging a strike.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:55.210
But, since March of this year, the US has
been using these reserves to alleviate gas

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:56.210
prices.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:02.280
Whether this is a justifiable reason is debateable,
but one thing that’s for sure is that the

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.140
US has been depleting these reserves fast.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:10.909
I could go through the specific numbers and
stuff, but I think just looking at this chart

00:01:10.909 --> 00:01:13.600
will tell you everything you need to know.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:19.080
So how much longer do we have until the US
depletes their backup reserves.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:25.110
THE COUNTDOWN:
To estimate how much time we have left, the

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:29.359
easiest thing to do would be to calculate
the average rate of decline and use that to

00:01:29.359 --> 00:01:31.180
project into the future.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:35.840
If you zoom into the chart, you’ll see that
the cliff dive really started in April of

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.100
2022 when the reserves stood at 568 million
barrels of oil.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:44.439
If we scroll over to October, however, the
reserves are estimated to stand at just 359

00:01:44.439 --> 00:01:45.439
million barrels.

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:51.119
That right there is not only a 40 year low
in terms of reserves but also a record pace

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:52.119
of decline.

00:01:52.119 --> 00:01:57.369
During this 6 month period, the US would’ve
have used up 209 million barrels of reserve

00:01:57.369 --> 00:01:58.369
oil.

00:01:58.369 --> 00:02:01.659
That translates to an average of about 35
million barrels per month.

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:06.759
At that rate, the reserves would only last
for another 10 months taking us to about August

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:07.920
of next year.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:12.390
But, if we look closer at the data, we’ll
see that we don’t have nearly that much

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:13.390
time.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:18.519
Here’s the thing, not all crude oil is created
equal and the US doesn’t just store one

00:02:18.519 --> 00:02:19.879
type of crude oil.

00:02:19.879 --> 00:02:23.650
They actually store two types called medium-sour
and light-sweet.

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:28.400
To the naked eye, both of these would look
the same as the differences are mainly internal.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.400
Medium-sour oil has a higher density and a
higher sulfur content which what the world

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.090
has become accustomed to.

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:39.580
This is the type of oil that is pumped out
of Russia, Venezuela and much of the Middle

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:40.580
East.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:44.250
And this is the type of oil that the US refiners
are used to.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:48.930
And given that refiners are currently the
biggest bottleneck in the oil market, the

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:50.739
US has leaned into their needs.

00:02:50.739 --> 00:02:56.560
In other words, the vast majority of oil the
US has been unloading as in 85% is medium

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.060
sour crude oil.

00:02:58.060 --> 00:03:02.460
This means that out of the 35 million barrels
that they were offloading every month, roughly

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:05.860
30 million barrels were sour and 5 million
were sweet.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:11.020
This wouldn’t be that big of a problem if
medium sour accounted for 85% of their reserves,

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:12.730
but it doesn’t.

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:17.760
According to government’s website, 40% of
their reserves were sweet and 60% were sour

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.050
as of the beginning of 2022.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:24.170
If we assume that this ratio was more or less
maintained until we saw the cliff dive in

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:29.330
April, we started with roughly 340 million
barrels of sour oil and 227 million barrels

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:30.660
of sweet oil.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:35.209
At a rate of 30 million barrels of sour oil
and 5 million barrels of sweet oil every month,

00:03:35.209 --> 00:03:40.579
we’ll be left with approximately 160 million
barrels of sour oil and 197 million barrels

00:03:40.579 --> 00:03:42.520
of sweet oil by October.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:47.519
If we don’t change up our rate of consumption
or ratio of consumption, the sour oil reserves

00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:48.799
will be gone by March.

00:03:48.799 --> 00:03:54.189
As for the sweet oil reserves, we’ll still
have plenty of that, but that probably wouldn’t

00:03:54.189 --> 00:03:55.189
be all that helpful.

00:03:55.189 --> 00:03:59.650
Here’s the thing, when you have a refining
bottle neck with sweet oil, pumping up the

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:02.379
supply won’t actually lead to more final
output.

00:04:02.379 --> 00:04:06.689
Also, it’s unlikely that refiners will be
willing to expand their light sweet operations

00:04:06.689 --> 00:04:10.680
all that much when this is just a temporary
influx of sweet oil.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:16.230
So, the countdown has already started, at
it’s just a matter of months until the US

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:19.750
will have to pull the plug.

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:24.340
RUGPULL:
Now, you might be thinking that this was a

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:26.830
nice thing for the government to do.

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:31.500
They finally stepped up and took action against
oil prices that were spiraling out of control.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:37.330
While that could definitely be the case, it’s
not that likely.

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:40.190
You see, the US never taps into this oil reserve.

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:45.460
Looking at this graph, you can see that the
reserves are always stagnating or going up.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:50.220
This is literally the first the time the US
has been depleting SPR reserves since it was

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.770
created way back in 1975.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:58.440
They didn’t even deplete the reserves in
2008 when the US saw a massive recession and

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:01.290
oil prices hit over $180 per barrel.

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:05.790
So, clearly, they’re more desperate than
ever before.

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:10.590
And why are they more desperate than ever
before, well it’s because inflation is way

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:15.390
out of control and its making the incumbent
politicians look incompetent right before

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:16.390
the mid term elections.

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:20.840
And that right there is the key to everything
they’ve been doing.

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:25.389
Obviously, there’s nothing the politicians
can do to bring inflation down to 3% or solve

00:05:25.389 --> 00:05:27.970
the supply shortage within the next two months.

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:32.010
But, something they can do is deplete the
emergency oil reserves to create the impression

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:33.840
that inflation is coming down.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:39.009
Here’s the thing, the average person doesn’t
actually follow CPI readings on a monthly

00:05:39.009 --> 00:05:40.009
basis.

00:05:40.009 --> 00:05:43.919
Their gauge of inflation is simply based on
how much their daily and monthly expenses

00:05:43.919 --> 00:05:44.919
are.

00:05:44.919 --> 00:05:49.229
If they suddenly go to the pump one day and
their bill is $10 lower than usual, they’re

00:05:49.229 --> 00:05:51.370
gonna assume that inflation is getting better.

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:54.600
It’s not just the pump that they’ll see
this difference either.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:59.139
When you bring down oil prices, you bring
down prices across the board because almost

00:05:59.139 --> 00:06:01.889
all everyday goods rely on transportation.

00:06:01.889 --> 00:06:03.020
Take milk prices for example.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:08.289
Milk is still super expensive on a historical
basis, but prices have stopped going up since

00:06:08.289 --> 00:06:09.289
May.

00:06:09.289 --> 00:06:13.699
We don’t know how much of this is actually
due to oil prices coming down, but one thing’s

00:06:13.699 --> 00:06:15.880
for sure, they’re definitely trying.

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:20.740
The only problem though is that all the signs
point to them stopping trying right after

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:22.720
the election is over.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:24.759
Take the oil depletion for example.

00:06:24.759 --> 00:06:28.889
If the rate of depletion was 15 or 20 million
barrels of oil per month, we could come to

00:06:28.889 --> 00:06:31.950
the conclusion that maybe its more of a long
term plan.

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:35.990
This rate would actually be sustainable for
a few years which might be enough time for

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:38.060
oil prices to naturally cool down.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:43.229
But, with a depletion rate of 35 million barrels
per month and sour oil reserves being projected

00:06:43.229 --> 00:06:48.250
to run out by Q2 of next year, it’s hard
to believe that this is anything but a short

00:06:48.250 --> 00:06:49.849
term political play.

00:06:49.849 --> 00:06:53.629
It seems like they wanted to bring down oil
prices as much as much as possible before

00:06:53.629 --> 00:06:59.379
the election, but after the election, it doesn’t
seem like they care all that much.

00:06:59.379 --> 00:07:03.259
Even if they did care, it’s not like they
could do anything about it given that the

00:07:03.259 --> 00:07:05.530
oil reserves would already be nearly depleted.

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:10.949
So, in my opinion, I think it’s highly likely
that they rugpull us shortly after the election,

00:07:10.949 --> 00:07:15.259
but I’ll let you be the judge of that.

00:07:15.259 --> 00:07:20.110
AFTERMATH:
Whether this is all just one big political

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:24.050
move or not, in the end, it doesn’t really
matter.

00:07:24.050 --> 00:07:29.580
Either way, we end up in the same destination
which is depleted emergency oil reserves.

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:31.660
So, what happens next?

00:07:31.660 --> 00:07:35.970
Well, fortunately for us, the US department
of Treasury has done some studies regarding

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:38.669
how the emergency reserves are affecting gas
prices.

00:07:38.669 --> 00:07:42.830
To accomplish this, they looked at data regarding
the stock of oil or the supply and the flow

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:43.830
of oil.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:49.110
Now of course, no model is perfect and that
applies to the US Treasury as well.

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:53.620
For example, in their calculations, they assumed
that there is zero elasticity on the supply

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:54.620
side.

00:07:54.620 --> 00:07:59.220
Similarly, they also assumed that logistical
and technical issues can be ignored.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:04.000
Given how bad the supply chain has been over
the past 2 years, these assumptions are obviously

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:07.909
not super realistic, but it’s the best that
we’ve got.

00:08:07.909 --> 00:08:12.539
According to the study, the release of American
oil reserves has resulted in a price reduction

00:08:12.539 --> 00:08:14.860
of 33 cents per gallon.

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:19.080
Something that we should note is that it wasn’t
just the US that was depleting their oil reserves

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:20.080
either.

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:23.879
When President Biden announced his plan to
leverage American reserves, he also got partners

00:08:23.879 --> 00:08:27.550
in the IEA or the International Energy Agency
to play along.

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:31.680
Together, it’s estimated that they’ve
brought down oil prices by approximately 0.38

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:32.920
center per gallon.

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:37.910
So, simple logic tells us that when these
organizations stop injecting oil in the system,

00:08:37.910 --> 00:08:43.190
gas prices should go up by 38 cents, but it’s
obviously not that simple.

00:08:43.190 --> 00:08:48.230
If oil prices naturally cool down by this
point, then maybe we can see oil prices continue

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:51.830
to trend down even after these guys withdraw
their support.

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:55.690
This would be the most ideal situation, and
hopefully, it’s what happens.

00:08:55.690 --> 00:09:00.710
If oil prices are still at relatively high
levels, however, we could easily see oil prices

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:05.030
spike by much more than 38 cents because we
would no longer have a safety net.

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:09.500
Oil suppliers will be well aware that the
US can’t pull off this stunt again, so they

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:13.340
might have even more pricing power than they
had before this fiasco.

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:17.500
The country that America would really have
to worry about is not Saudi Arabia or some

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:21.640
other Middle Eastern country, they would actually
have to worry about Canada.

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:26.210
Most people don’t know this, but Canada
is actually America’s number 1 sources of

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:27.210
imported oil.

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:33.040
Literally 62% of US crude oil imports comes
from Canada, so they have quite a bit of power.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:38.340
Fortunately, the US and Canada are pretty
good allies, so it’s extremely unlikely

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:41.000
that Canada would do anything to destroy America.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:45.790
For example, they’re not gonna stop oil
exports altogether and watch as gas prices

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:48.440
shoot up to $30 per gallon in America.

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:53.250
But, just because they wont do something so
drastic doesn’t mean that they’ll be gracious

00:09:53.250 --> 00:09:59.080
with pricing because America has never been
gracious with pricing when it comes to Canadian

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:00.080
oil.

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:03.400
You see, Canada actually has the third largest
oil reserves in the entire world.

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:07.160
But, they’ve never really been able to leverage
this to become a global player.

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:12.270
If you’re interested in why, I have a full
video about Canada’s oil situation.

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:18.030
But, long story short, Canada has been forced
into a situation where 99% of their oil ends

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:21.420
up in the North American market or basically
just America.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:25.460
And this has given the US the opportunity
to basically blackmail Canada into giving

00:10:25.460 --> 00:10:26.910
them cheap oil.

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:31.460
The argument from the US is usually, we have
these massive reserves as well sources from

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:33.290
Russia and the Middle East.

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:39.750
So, we don’t need you, you need us, so if
you want us to buy your oil, give us a discount.

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:42.970
And that’s exactly what Canada’s been
doing with the average discount on Canadian

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:44.540
oil being $20 per barrel.

00:10:44.540 --> 00:10:47.330
But, now the table have turned.

00:10:47.330 --> 00:10:52.640
The emergency reserves are rapidly depleting
and Russia is out of the equation, so Canada

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:54.350
has the upper hand.

00:10:54.350 --> 00:10:58.830
And honestly, after getting taken advantage
of for so many decades, I think it’s only

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:03.820
fair that Canada gets to turn the script on
the US, but let’s just say, it wont be very

00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:06.710
fun for our pocket books.

00:11:06.710 --> 00:11:11.580
GAS PRICES:
In the end, if you’ve been wondering why

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:15.940
gas prices have been plummeting over the past
few months, it’s not because inflation is

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:16.940
coming down.

00:11:16.940 --> 00:11:21.050
It’s actually because the government has
essentially been handing out gas stimulus

00:11:21.050 --> 00:11:22.060
from their emergency reserves.

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:27.300
At the current rate, however, they wont be
able to sustain this stimulus for much longer,

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:31.350
but honestly, that seems to have been their
plan all along.

00:11:31.350 --> 00:11:35.580
Cool down gas prices before the election as
much as possible and we’ll worry about the

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:36.580
rest later.

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:42.510
In the medium term, this may end up giving
Canada more pricing power than ever, allowing

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:47.460
them to finally have some time in the spotlight
at the expense of everyday Americans.

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:52.230
Again, I wouldn’t blame Canada for this,
but rather American politicians for putting

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:54.040
America in such a situation.

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:56.150
But, that’s just what I think.

00:11:56.150 --> 00:11:59.670
Do you think it was justified to leverage
the emergency oil reserves?

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:01.090
Comment that down below.

00:12:01.090 --> 00:12:05.000
Also, drop a like if you wish politicians
didn’t just care about elections.

